{"name":"Herbal Tea","hex":"#f9fee9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"herbal tea","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A beverage made by infusing mainly the leaves (and sometimes the flowers, roots, or stems) of various herbs or other plants, generally not including true tea (Camellia sinensis)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/herbal_tea"}],"entries":[{"title":"Herbal tea","blocks":[{"text":"Herbal teas or herb teas, technically known as herbal infusions and less commonly called tisanes, are beverages made from the infusion or decoction in water of any herbs, spices, or other plant material. Many herbs used in teas/tisanes are also used in herbal medicine and in folk medicine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Hibiscus_Delight_tisane.jpg/330px-Hibiscus_Delight_tisane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbal_tea","wikidata":"Q379932"},{"title":"Herbal","blocks":[{"text":"A herbal is a book containing the names and descriptions of plants, usually with information on their medicinal, tonic, culinary, toxic, hallucinatory, aromatic, or magical powers, and the legends associated with them. A herbal may also classify the plants it describes, may give recipes for herbal extracts, tinctures, or potions, and sometimes include mineral and animal medicaments in addition to those obtained from plants. Herbals were often illustrated to assist plant identification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Dioscorides_De_Materia_Medica_Byzantium_15th_century.jpg/330px-Dioscorides_De_Materia_Medica_Byzantium_15th_century.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbal","wikidata":"Q6165948"},{"title":"Tea","blocks":[{"text":"Tea is an aromatic beverage prepared by infusion of hot or boiling fresh water with fresh or cured leaves of Camellia sinensis, an evergreen shrub native to East Asia that originated in the borderlands of southwestern China, northeast India and northern Myanmar. Tea has a stimulant effect in humans primarily due to its caffeine content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Longjing_tea_steeping_in_gaiwan.jpg/330px-Longjing_tea_steeping_in_gaiwan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tea","wikidata":"Q6097"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Butterfly-pea_flower_tea_3.jpg/500px-Butterfly-pea_flower_tea_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Butterfly-pea_flower_tea_3.jpg/1280px-Butterfly-pea_flower_tea_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Butterfly-pea_flower_tea_3.jpg/500px-Butterfly-pea_flower_tea_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Butterfly-pea flower tea. The one on the right has had lime juice added, making it turn purple.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AButterfly-pea_flower_tea_3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Paul_Berthon_-_Tisane_Gauloise.jpg/500px-Paul_Berthon_-_Tisane_Gauloise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Paul_Berthon_-_Tisane_Gauloise.jpg/500px-Paul_Berthon_-_Tisane_Gauloise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A promotional french poster for \"Tisane Gauloise\", by Paul Berthon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APaul_Berthon_-_Tisane_Gauloise.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/LE_POINT_DE_VUE11n4000.j